{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":601.3499755859375,"text":"that requires the accountable executive, data lineage, dependencies across source systems, validation results, escalation path, and the kill switch runbook. Most importantly, it documents your regression triggers, which are any updates to our prompt, database model, or source API that require full revalidation before running in production again. Your third artifact, this is going to be the autonomy scorecard. The steering committee uses it to grant autonomy in four stages. Stage one is shadow mode. The analysts build the report as they do today. The agent runs right alongside them and the team compares the two. Stage two is the supervised draft. The agent compiles the first draft and the senior analyst reviews every single line item. Stage three is the exception and sample, where the analyst reviews lagged initiatives plus a random sample of the clean. Then we have stage four, where it's exceptions only. The analyst reviews only high-risk anomalies supported by periodic independent sampling. Pay close attention to stage four, however, because once humans only look at exceptions, your biggest vulnerability is what the agent failed to flag. So I advise tracking missed material issues through independent sampling, monitoring error severity, and watching your override rate carefully. A falling override rate does not automatically mean the agent is getting smarter. It can also mean the depth of review has split. So independent sampling tells you which one of these you are seeing. The scorecard puts those control metrics side by side with your value metrics, cycle time, analyst hours deployed, and your detection time. And with that single-page view, the steering committee can make clean, efficient data-backed decisions to either promote the agent, hold it where it is, step it back, or down to supervised review stage. So let's come back to the three questions. Ask them of every agent you build, fund, or govern. What value does it create? Who is accountable for it? And what evidence permits it to operate with autonomy? In our M&A example today, the agent does not magically create the $400 million in synergies. Procurement, supply chain, and operating leaders capture that value through the gritty work of integration. But what the agent delivers is clear. Verified evidence weeks ahead of schedule, giving leadership the runway to intervene while the deal's synergies can still be captured. Building an agent that works is just the first step. Answering those three questions is what earns it the trust to run at scale. Thank you. ♪ Hi, I'm Steve Jamieson, AI Research and Engineering Leader at Jellyfish. I'm here to show you why tokens are the cheap part. The research that I'll present is drawn from the Jellyfish platform, the industry's largest set of engineering workflow data joined to AI telemetry data. It's no secret that AI costs are climbing. They're up 60x from this time last year. The use cases are wide and varied. The models and harnesses are getting much better. The implications are exciting. But discussions around budget and spend are not as much fun. These conversations involve your peers and senior leadership and the board, not always the easiest audiences, especially when you don't have the right data. And it's real money these days. Output is up too. Merged PRs per engineer are up 43% over the same one-year horizon. This is phenomenal. We've not seen productivity inputs like this, especially on this timescale. There's plenty of research and anecdotal data to know that AI and agentic coding are driving these throughput gains. Heck, even our charts show the correlation. Notice where the inflection points are? AI spend increased in early 2026, and throughput gains start a steady increase of their own. There's a tension with these two numbers, though. How do you reconcile them? Spend is up 60x. Throughput is also up 43%. Is that good? Bad? 43% is a big number, but so is 60x. How do you know if you're spending enough? Is there more productivity gain locked behind a checkbook? Should we increase spend 50%, 2x more? Or how do you know if you spent too much? I'm sure many of you have felt the tension here. The CFO or board are asking for the receipts of the spend. Can you show them? Will merged PRs that are up 43% satisfy them? Also, what happens if you cap use and spend? Are you stopping from setting tokens on fire? Or are you stunting further efficiency gains? You can ask peers at other companies what they're paying. For many, it's still a moving target, though. But how do you equate that to your org? Surely, just comparing their bill to yours isn't adequate. We've heard the reports of companies spending 100k, 500k, even millions on AI. Setting, then resetting their AI budgets as reality sets in. Ultimately, these two numbers can't tell the story. And even together, they missed the biggest line on the cost side. These two numbers leave out the biggest cost of all, developer salary. AI is making developers more productive on a scale we haven't seen before. It isn't just another tool, it's intelligence. This intelligence dramatically changes how much each dev can produce. That's why we need to think about its cost differently. Not off to the side as overhead, but as part of what it takes to produce each PR. We're going to call this intelligence costs of a PR. What you pay your engineers, plus what you pay for AI, put them together in one cost bucket. AI is about 2.5% of the cost to produce another unit of merge code. Salary is the other 97.5%. This is in line with other reported figures. And this data is from August 2026. Here, tokens are the cheap part. But that's current relative costs, not dollars. Let's look at what happened to the cost of a merge PR over the last year. The dollar cost of a merge PR fell 26% over the last year. Not PRs created, but PRs merged. Review time is factored in, and some level of quality gates has been cleared. That decline in cost per merge PR includes a 60X increase in AI costs over the same timeframe. The change is nearly universal too. 83% of the companies we studied showed a drop in cost per PR. What does this tell us? The additional throughput gains are spreading salary thinner across PRs, and this is happening faster than the AI costs are increasing. What's the range in tokens spent? We ranked companies by token usage, then grouped them into quintiles, or fifths. The range here is quite large. The lightest group, about 0.3% of the cost of a PR, is AI. This represents about 60 million tokens per dev per month. The top 20% of AI spend per dev, we're going to call the heaviest spend group. There, 8.5% of the PR cost is AI. This represents about 2.5 billion tokens per dev per month, about two orders of magnitude more than the lightest group. We'll use these groups on the next slides to see how token spend influences other metrics. First, PR throughput measured as merged PRs per dev. Split by the same.","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":0.0,"end":10.0,"text":" that requires the accountable executive, data lineage, dependencies across source systems,","tokens":[50364,300,7029,264,18024,10140,11,1412,38257,11,36606,2108,4009,3652,11,50864],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.4001093804836273,"compression_ratio":1.4450867176055908,"no_speech_prob":0.12919382750988007},{"id":1,"seek":0,"start":10.0,"end":16.0,"text":" validation results, escalation path, and the kill switch runbook.","tokens":[50864,24071,3542,11,17871,399,3100,11,293,264,1961,3679,1190,2939,13,51164],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.4001093804836273,"compression_ratio":1.4450867176055908,"no_speech_prob":0.12919382750988007},{"id":2,"seek":0,"start":16.0,"end":23.0,"text":" Most importantly, it documents your regression triggers, which are any updates to our prompt,","tokens":[51164,4534,8906,11,309,8512,428,24590,22827,11,597,366,604,9205,281,527,12391,11,51514],"temperature":0.0